## Running Multiple MCP Servers

Your `.vscode/mcp.json` can define as many servers as you need. They all run simultaneously and their tools are all available to Copilot Agent Mode at the same time.

```json
{
  "servers": {
    "github": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-github"],
      "env": {
        "GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN": "${env:GITHUB_TOKEN}"
      }
    },
    "atlassian": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@atlassian/mcp-atlassian"],
      "env": {
        "JIRA_URL": "https://your-domain.atlassian.net",
        "JIRA_USERNAME": "${env:JIRA_USER}",
        "JIRA_API_TOKEN": "${env:JIRA_TOKEN}"
      }
    },
    "postgres": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-postgres", "${env:DATABASE_URL}"]
    }
  }
}
```

Notice `${env:VARIABLE_NAME}` — this pulls values from environment variables rather than hardcoding credentials in the JSON file. This is the secure pattern: set sensitive values as environment variables in your shell profile or `.env` file, reference them in `mcp.json`, and you can safely commit `mcp.json` to version control.

**VS Code reads `${env:}` at server startup** — no extra configuration needed.

**Setting environment variables securely:**
```bash
# Add to your .bashrc, .zshrc, or PowerShell profile
export GITHUB_TOKEN="ghp_your_token_here"
export JIRA_TOKEN="your_jira_api_token"
export DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:pass@localhost/mydb"
```
